he logrotate config persisted, the timer remained active/enabled, and a fresh forced rotation plus write succeeded, demonstrating full functional recovery of the configured rotation behavior after reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8aa48e0eacdc4cbd\",\"cmd-0a01bdd5eb1a4f87\",\"cmd-ac6c05988102467c\",\"cmd-56fce067a7ed4fb0\",\"cmd-6845eb4ea2404046\",\"global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.97,\"summary\":\"All 8 executor commands have a clear purpose: two are read-only discovery/inspection (cmd-48ce0161be9c4a4c, cmd-ce5833190e1040c3), one writes and validates the logrotate config plus exercises two forced rotation cycles to prove the setup works (cmd-f2902665c2964f7e), one captures the mandatory pre-restart baseline (cmd-8aa48e0eacdc4cbd), the reboot and its acknowledgement/timeout pair are the mandatory restart action (cmd-0a01bdd5eb1a4f87, cmd-ac6c05988102467c), one confirms the new boot identity (cmd-56fce067a7ed4fb0), and the final command performs mandatory post-restart recovery verification (cmd-6845eb4ea2404046). No unnecessary or unreverted mutations were found: the only state changes are the intended logrotate config file, the intentionally forced log rotations (an expected artifact of validating the rotation scheme), and the reboot itself. Failed-services and package-health checks are clean and unchanged in both fixed snapshots; listening-socket and /tmp differences are attributable solely to the reboot (new boot-id-scoped systemd-private dirs, refreshed PIDs) and are not executor residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-48ce0161be9c4a4c\",\"cmd-ce5833190e1040c3\",\"cmd-f2902665c2964f7e\",\"global:after_prepare:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_prepare:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor configured /etc/logrotate.d/inventory-app with 